#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128885 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d4c1a7b-2341-5b8c-8112-0290f8eda65f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Block Diagonalization of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "1cc03cef-e717-5ac1-ab9d-75508f3aa056" :question/math-academy-id "e-5533" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B$, where the $2 \\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PBP^{-1}$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}}$, and two complex eigenvalues\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nthen $A=PBP^{-1}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}λ_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & Re(λ_{2}) & Im(λ_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -Im(λ_{2}) & Re(λ_{2})\\end{bmatrix}$\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P$.\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A$, we solve the characteristic equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A - λI) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}-2 - λ & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 - λ & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(-1 - λ)((-2 - λ)^{2} - 1(-1)) &= 0 \\\\\n(-1 - λ)(λ^{2} + 4λ + 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1={\\color{red}-1}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ_{2, 3} &= (-4 ± \\sqrt{(4)^{2} - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 5})/(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-4 ± \\sqrt{-4}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-4 ± 2\\textrm{i}}{2} \\\\\n&=-2 ± \\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128886 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a65788-ff15-58e9-8329-c69b2a8e311b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "fb7c97e6-f106-5a47-ad01-75205c0d2603" :question/math-academy-id "e-5534" :question/problem "Consider the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2 \\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=2$ and $\\lambda_2=1-\\textrm{i}$, respectively.\nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1}$, find the matrix $P$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}}$, and two complex eigenvalues\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nthen $A=PBP^{-1}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}λ_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & Re(λ_{2}) & Im(λ_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -Im(λ_{2}) & Re(λ_{2})\\end{bmatrix}$\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P$.\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${{\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}}}$, and the second and third columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128887 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ccd12c-a814-5ebb-985c-eef8707001f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of an NxN Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "2b2a7fbc-8911-507f-915c-cf9b50653e5f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5535" :question/problem "Consider the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2 \\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 + 2\\textrm{i} \\\\ -1 + 3\\textrm{i} \\\\ 4 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ -\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=7 + 4\\textrm{i}$ and $\\lambda_3=-3 + 2\\textrm{i}$, respectively.\nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1}$, find a possible matrix $P$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\lambda_1}=\\overline{\\color{red}\\lambda}_{\\color{red}2} \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad {\\color{blue}\\lambda_3}=\\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} = \\overline{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{red}2} \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nthen $A=PBP^{-1}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}Re(λ_{1}) & Im(λ_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -Im(λ_{1}) & Re(λ_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & Re(λ_{3}) & Im(λ_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -Im(λ_{3}) & Re(λ_{3})\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$[\\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; Re(v_{1})\\begin{vmatrix}Im(v_{1}) & Re(v_{3})\\end{vmatrix}Im(v_{3}); \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid]$\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P$.\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}}$, and the third and forth columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3}$. Therefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128913 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6704692a-2219-559f-9352-97c5fb590894" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Block Diagonal Form"}] :question/id #uuid "da515ea7-f4b0-58c5-adab-c826563dd3cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5906" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2 \\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1}$, what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A$?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}}$, and two complex eigenvalues\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nthen $A=PBP^{-1}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}λ_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & Re(λ_{2}) & Im(λ_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -Im(λ_{2}) & Re(λ_{2})\\end{bmatrix}$\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P$.\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_{1,2}=1 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\color{red}\\lambda_3=-3}$.\n- From our matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4∓\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1 ± \\textrm{i} \\\\ 1 ± 2\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a65788-ff15-58e9-8329-c69b2a8e311b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Invertible Matrix in Block Diagonal Form of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaa2a88c-ee4e-5552-800a-8a6f56286ea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127539} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d262e2-54b6-58ca-8918-35bbc90f5311" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127540} {:answer/id #uuid "34076a5c-5158-5d58-8dcf-3e7a0a7211cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127541} {:answer/id #uuid "4136254b-a043-564b-b64f-714354cf87df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127542} {:answer/id #uuid "662b5a3a-2801-529f-af8f-ebf574b450e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "662b5a3a-2801-529f-af8f-ebf574b450e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127543} :answer-field/id #uuid "9678063e-8cb2-50fb-b9c5-b7e752c39105" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127538}] :question/id #uuid "97813176-8f27-5690-98e4-6d6c62e9f0d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-38660" :question/problem "Consider the vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2 \\cdot 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 + 7\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ \\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $λ_{1} =-1 - 2\\textrm{i}$ and $λ_{3} =-2$, respectively.\nGiven that $A = PBP^{-1}$, which of the following could be the matrix $P$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6704692a-2219-559f-9352-97c5fb590894" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Block Diagonal Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3b009c1-7a89-5fb9-90b1-5f0301f2b085" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-2 ± 5\\textrm{i}$ and $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 ± 2\\textrm{i} \\\\ ∓\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127557} {:answer/id #uuid "da9dd9f8-6e2a-58f1-aafc-d18d710fbba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-2 ± 5\\textrm{i}$ and $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ ∓2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1∓\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127558} {:answer/id #uuid "db3dd04d-3f69-5dbf-8727-718190d44489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 5 ± 2\\textrm{i}$ and $v = \\begin{bmatrix}2\\textrm{i} \\\\ ∓2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1∓\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127559} {:answer/id #uuid "0aadbaac-e390-52d1-9a4f-7010d75a8e91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 1 ± 5\\textrm{i}$ and $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ ∓2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1∓\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127560} {:answer/id #uuid "1851a87f-94da-5012-a5c7-9ab10c5bfd99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 5 ± 2\\textrm{i}$ and $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ ± 2 \\\\ 1∓\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da9dd9f8-6e2a-58f1-aafc-d18d710fbba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-2 ± 5\\textrm{i}$ and $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ ∓2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1∓\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127558} :answer-field/id #uuid "94fb4a67-337e-5942-962e-975b3340bbcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127556}] :question/id #uuid "582f937c-6cfb-53b4-966a-6cea9b2086e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-38665" :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2 \\cdot 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A = PBP^{-1}$, what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A$?"}]}
